hey’re probably feeling just as out of place. ### 4. Move toward interdisciplinary or industry math settings Pure mathematics departments tend to concentrate the exact personality type you’re describing. Applied math, statistics, data science, biostatistics, or financial math often attract people with broader interests and more varied social skills. The culture in those environments can be less all-consuming. If she’s early in her career, she might consider steering her path toward a setting that suits her personality better. ### 5. Use her difference strategically If she’s more socially comfortable than her peers, she has a rare asset. She can be the one who communicates clearly, mentors students well, collaborates smoothly, and presents at conferences without mumbling at the board. That can become her professional calling card. She might even be deliberately slotted into roles that require a “normal” face—department outreach, teaching, interdisciplinary projects—which can raise her visibility and job satisfaction. ### 6. Consider that “fitting in” might not be possible—and decide accordingly If the environment is truly homogenous and her difference is causing constant friction or subtle exclusion, she might need to weigh whether staying is worth it. There’s no shame in moving to a workplace or subfield where she can both do math and feel like herself. Life’s too short to spend it as a social outsider in your own profession. The stereotype is real, and your cousin is up against it. The solution isn’t to pretend it’s not there, or to pressure her to become more nerdy. It’s to treat the situation realistically and help her build a life—professionally and personally—where she can be both a mathematician and herself without penalty.
